A||N||10||feffer||lowered oil pressure||||1||
Z||000000||feffer||05-26-2000||06:49 PM||||	I just changed the oil and filter in my 87 2.5L (with the filter inside the pan) and now the pressure is way lower than before the change.... Out of boredom while waiting for every drop of oil to drain (this filter is such a mess), I actually read the oil change procedure in the Haynes manual. It mentions (on page 258, in the supplemental section) checking to make sure the o-ring has come out when the filter was removed, and coating the new o-ring and sliding it into position, THEN the new filter. Now, I've only had the car a little while (and changed the oil only once before, right when I got the car), but I didn't do that last time. I simply looked at the filter I took out and put the new one in the same way, which was with the o-ring already on the filter (the way it came out of the box), in that ridge about an inch from what turns out to be the bottom of the filter when it's installed.<BR>	Anyway, this time I took that ring off and put it up there like the manual says. My pressure gauge used to ride at about 60 psi and would come down a little when the car was idling. Today, it was about 40 at startup, then hovered in that space between the red danger zone and 20 psi and would drop into the red when the car idled. I'm wondering what IS the right place for that o-ring? Moving it was the likely cause for the change in pressure, right? I used the same oil-Valvoline 5W30, AC filter PF1072 which is equivalent to the FRAM PH3970 I took out. Should I be worried about this low pressure or should I really have been worried before when it was 60 psi? If so, was any harm done driving the car that way for who knows how long before?||||reg||1
Z||000001||triker||05-26-2000||07:21 PM||||How many miles on your engine?  I doubt if anyone on the forum would dissagree if I said you should be using at least 10w 30, especially this time of year.  In my oppinion the 5w was a way of increasing gas milage at the expense of engine life.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||05-26-2000||08:53 PM||||I agree with triker.  When I had an '84 the oil pressure was similar to the lower readings.  And I agree the thinner oils were used to boost milage.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||05-26-2000||11:11 PM||||I've put 10W-40 in my GT year round, except when I lived in Minnesota and Connecticut, when it was 5W-30 in the winter, 10W-30 in fall and spring.  It rarely gets below freezing here in SE Georgia.  No problems in 165,000 miles.||||reg||1
Z||000004||turbo86se||05-27-2000||10:43 AM||||My 88 coupe has the same symptoms. I've <BR>got 118,000 miles on the engine.  At <BR>least you're not the only one!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  The <BR>only cure I know of is this, <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=226077&a=1676320&p=20236934&Sequence=0" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=226077&a=1676320&p=20236934&Sequence=0</A> <BR>Yank that tired old 4-cyl out in install this 56K mile V6 from an 88 Formula that my mechanic converted into a 3.8<BR>That's this winter!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||05-27-2000||01:10 PM||||Don't use 5W-30. Unless it's winter with temps below -10 you don't need it. I live in ILL and in winter its 10W-40 summer is 20W-50. 5W-30 is great on newer vehicles that have tighter tolerences when the engine was built, but on Fiero engines stick with 10W-30 or better. In my opinion 5W-30 is only slightly better than water.<BR>TuboSE, How did your mechanic convert the 88 Formula V-6 2.8L into a 3.8L? <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||feffer||05-27-2000||09:47 PM||||I kind of figured I'd hear all about using 10W30 instead (especially after following all the threads about low oil pressure already on here). My engine was a replacement from a donor when the original bit the dust. All I was told was that it had about 90K on it. Whether or not I believe it, that's all I got to go on.<BR>So, point taken about the heavier weight oil. But the fact remains I used the same 5W30 BEFORE as after this oil change, and the pressure is what changed. The question is: did I put the filter o-ring in the right place?||||reg||5
Z||000007||Cooter||05-27-2000||09:54 PM||||If in doubt, pull it out. Cheaper than replacing the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||05-28-2000||05:57 PM||||The only difference between 5w-30w and 10w-30w is that 5w-30w flows more easily in colder temperatures than 10w-30w does. At operating temperatures they have the same viscosity ratings. Using 10w-30w engine oil is a waste of your money and offers less protection for your engine upon startup when it is cold because it takes more time for 10w-30w to get flowing. AGAIN THEY BOTH HAVE THE SAME VISCOSITY (oil thickness) RATINGS AT OPERATING TEMPERATURE (195 degrees F).<P>Feffer, the O-ring on the oil filter inside your engine (force balancer 4-cylinder engine) must be leaking internally causing your engine oil pressure to drop. Get another oil filter and don’t remove that o-ring, just lube it with engine oil with your finger and push the new filter onto the force balancer. If you continue to drive with low pressure you could ruin your engine.<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phaeton||05-28-2000||06:41 PM||||5w(winter)  cold flow<BR>30 hot flow<BR>the second number is for engine operating temperature, the 'w' number is cold startup.<P>Conventional oil has additive packets to give multi grade capability.<BR>Synthetics are built that way.<BR>IMHO running conventional oil is very similar to running bias ply tires, good in very limited situations.||||reg||12
Z||000010||fiero56||05-29-2000||11:59 PM||||I have 160,000 miles on my unrebuilt 2.8.  It will soon be getting hot here again, and I am wondering what weight oil I should start using, or should I just stick with the 5w30 I have been using? Maybe 10w40? It still gets fairly cool here at night, low 40s if that affects anything.||||reg||1
